sions 51\n\nArivazhagan Rajendran, Yue Li,\n\nMasayuki Endo &\n\nHiroshi Sugiyama\n\nG-quadruplex structures have been mainly investigated\n\nby the indirect bulk techniques like spectroscopy, gel\n\nelectrophoresis, calorimetry, kinetic methods, and so on.\n\nAlthough these techniques are effective for the investi-\n\ngations of quadruplex structures and quadruplex-binding\n\nligands/proteins, they often fail to extract enough infor-\n\nmation on the events that occur in single-molecule. For\n\ninstance, G-rich sequences may form a mixture of struc-\n\ntures that significantly differ in strand polarity (parallel,\n\nantiparallel or mixed) and stoichiometry (one, two, three\n\nor four stands), and bulk studies produce an average sig-\n\nnal from these mixed structures. To overcome these prob-\n\nlems, direct and single-molecule analyses are necessary.\n\nSingle-molecule fluorescence resonance energy transfer,\n\noptical microscopy, and forced-based methods such as\n\noptical and magnetic tweezers are powerful tools to study\n\nthe single-molecule dynamics, while it is difficult to widely\n\napply these techniques for the investigation of a particu-\n\nlar G-quadruplex conformation. We have recently utilized\n\na DNA origami nanostructure for the direct visualization\n\nc\n\n2015 Future Science Ltd 39\n\nRajendran, Li, Endo & Sugiyama\n\n\nFurther, the available single-molecule techniques are not effective for the\n\ninvestigation of intermolecular G-quadruplexes with control over strand\n\npolarity and stoichiometry. Thus, a novel single-method method for the\n\nanalysis of intermolecular quadruplelxes is urgently needed. To circumvent\n\nthese problems, we have recently carried out a series of direct and real-time\n\nanalysis on the salt- [9,15], ligand- [16] and protein-induced [17] formation\n\nof G-quadruplexes. For this study, we utilized a DNA origami frame as a\n\nnanoscaffold to control the strand polarity and stoichiometry, and direct\n\nvisualization was carried out using high-speed atomic force microscopy (HS-\n\nAFM) [18–20]. These investigations are briefly outlined here.\n\nDNA origami frame & types of G-quadruplexes investigated\n\nFor the analysis of G-quadruplexes, we adopted a DNA origami [21,22]frame\n\n(Figure 3.1) that is originally developed for the analysis of enzymatic reac-\n\ntions [23]. This frame contains an inner vacant space of about 40 ×40 nm\n\nin which two sets of connection sites (termed A–B and C–D) were intro-\n\nduced to hybridize the duplex DNAs that contain G-repeat regions. Each\n\nconnecting site is a single-stranded DNA loop which is designed as part of\n\nthe DNA origami by extending the M13mp18 scaffold and the length of each\n\nsite is 32-mer. The distance between two connection sites (e.g., A and B) is\n\ndesigned to be 64-mer double-stranded DNA, which corresponds to a length\n\nof about 22 nm. A lacking-corner purposely kept at the right bottom of the\n\nframe enables the identification of the orientation of the frame. By attach-\n\ning the DNA sequences of interest, we could control the strand polarity and\n\nstoichiometry. Also by confining the DNA sequences within the nanospace,\n\nwe could increase the local concentration of the sequences and achieve the\n\ntarget structure with reasonably good yield.\n\nWe have investigated two types of structures: i) a (3 + 1)-type [9]and\n\nii) a tetramolecular antiparallel [15–17] G-quadruplex structures. For the\n\n(3 + 1)-type structures, we utilized two Watson-Crick duplexes, each con-\n\ntaining a single-stranded G-rich protrusion, with three G-tracts in the upper\n\nwww.future-science.com 41\n\nRajendran, Li, Endo & Sugiyama\n\nFigure 3.1 The design of the DNA origami frame and the insertion of the duplexes containing G-G\n\nmismatch repeats in the middle.\n\n∼18 nm\n\n(7 duplexes)(9 duplexes)\n\n~44 nm (128 bp)\n\n(16 duplexes)\n\n(80 bp)\n\n~28 nm ~28 nm\n\n(80 bp)\n\n~80 nm\n\n~40 nm ~23 nm\n\n(32 duplexes)\n\n~100 nm (288 bp)\n\