y of the G-strands is necessary to bring the G-rich\n\nMore-\n\nover, we have attached the DNA strands of interest within a DNA origami\n\nframe and we could successfully control the strand polarity, whereas it is\n\ndifficult to control using other methods of analysis. In the absence of any\n\nprevious structural information on tetramolecular G-quadruplex structures,\n\nour analysis becomes important to understand these structures and their\n\ndynamics in aqueous buffer conditions.\n\nLigand-induced quadruplex formation\n\nFor the ligand-induced G-quadruplex formation [16], we have adopted the\n\nsame set of sequences that we have used for the salt-induced formation of\n\ntetramolecular G-quadruplex. For the quadruplex-binding ligand, we used\n\nbisquinolinium pyridine dicarboxamide compound bearing a linker termi-\n\nnated by biotin (pyrido-dicarboxamide [PDC]–biotin, Figure 3.5 A). As we\n\nhave shown above, the incorporated duplexes are unable to form a quadru-\n\nplex structure in the absence of quadruplex-promoting salts or ligands. Thus,\n\nthe topography of the incorporated strands in the AFM image was paral-\n\nlel. Once we added the PDC-biotin ligand, the X-shape was seen clearly\n\nin several instances (see representative images in Figure 3.5 B, top panel)\n\nwhen the image was recorded for the long-strands with six G-repeats. In\n\nthis case, the yield of X-shapes in the presence of PDC–biotin ligand (1 ␮M)\n\nwas estimated to be 58% and this yield was further increased when we used\n\nhigher concentration of the ligand (73% in 10 ␮M of PDC-biotin). All other\n\nsequences tested were also found to undergo the topographical change\n\nfrom the parallel to X-shape with significant yield (Figure 3.5 B, top panel).\n\nThese observations clearly indicate that the PDC–biotin ligand induced the\n\nformation of a four-stranded G-quadruplex.\n\nIn order to confirm the ligand-induced quadruplex formation and binding\n\nof the ligand at the quadruplex region, we recorded the images in the\n\nsimultaneous presence of ligand and streptavidin (STV). The biotin moiety\n\npresent in the ligand can be used to bind STV, which in turn can be used\n\nas a pixel enhancing marker in AFM to identify the presence of ligand. The\n\npurified origami assembly was incubated with PDC–biotin and immobilized\n\non the mica surface. STV was added on the surface and incubated for 5 min,\n\nand imaged after removal of excess STV on the surface. Images recorded\n\nfor the long-strand with all three types of G–G mismatches are shown in\n\nFigure 3.5 B, bottom panel. The bright spots seen in the images represent\n\nSTV, and these bright spots were always observed in the middle of the X-\n\nshape. There was no bright spot observed on the duplex or origami core,\n\nindicating that the ligand was bound to the quadruplex structure. Moreover,\n\nthis observation evidenced that PDC–biotin ligand selectively binds to the\n\nG-quadruplex structure over the duplex DNA. Further, the height analysis\n\nwww.future-science.com 47\n\nRajendran, Li, Endo & Sugiyama\n\nFigure 3.5 (A) PDC–biotin ligand. (B) Representative zoom-in AFM images of the\n\nDNA origami frames with incorporated duplexes recorded in the presence of\n\nPDC-biotin ligand alone (top panel) and simultaneous presence of ligand and STV\n\n(bottom panel).\n\nN NH\n\nNH\n\nO N\n\nHN O O O N\n\nH\n\nHN\n\nHN\n\nO\n\nN\n\nO\n\nS\n\nO\n\nH\n\nH\n\n++\n\nLinker\n\nBiotin\n\nPDC\n\nA\n\nB\n\nLong-strand, PDC-biotin, no STV\n\nLong-strand, PDC-biotin and STV\n\n4G 3G 6G\n\n3G 6G\n\n(X-shape with STV in the middle)\n\n(X-shap e)\n\nL\n\non\n\ng\n\n-\n\ns\n\nt\n\nr\n\na\n\nn\n\nd\n\n,\n\nP\n\nD\n\nC\n\n-\n\nb\n\n(\n\nX-\n\ns\n\nh\n\na\n\np\n\ne\n\n4G\n\nAll images were recorded for 67-mer top and 77-mer bottom duplexes. Image\n\nsize: 125 ×125 nm; [Tris-HCl] = 20 mM, pH 7.6; [MgCl 2] = 10 mM; [EDTA] = 1 mM;\n\n\nsingle-molecule level will strengthen the understanding on the chaperone\n\nactivity of HIV-1 nucleocapsid proteins. This in turn will be helpful for the\n\ndevelopment of drugs both targeting the G-quadruplexes and also against\n\nAIDS.\n\nBesides the advantages of our method, it has few drawbacks such as surface\n\nimmobilization of the origami assembly and the sample surface interac-\n\ntion that may influence the formation dynamics of the G-quadruplexes. The\n\nspatial resolution of nanometer is not sufficient to get the structural infor-\n\nmation beyond what we have described here. The origami based method\n\nis very convenient analytical tool for the analysis of model sequences while\n\nit is difficult to extend its application for the analysis inside a cell. Further\n\ndevelopments are necessary for the wide application of this method that\n\nincludes cellular analysis.\n\nConclusions\n\nWe have successfully developed a novel single-molecule method using a\n\nDNA origami nanoscaffold for the label-free direct observation of the for-\n\nmation of G-quadruplex structures. Using this method, the salt-induced for-\n\nmation of a (3 + 1)-type and a tetramolecular antiparallel G-quadruplexes\n\nwere investigated. Also, the ligand-induced formation of the tetramolec-\n\nular G-quadruplex was also studied with control over the strand polarity\n\nand stoichiometry. This structural control and analysis of a single quadru-\n\nplex of interest makes our method superior than the bulk methods that\n\nusually analyze a mixture of structures. Further, the molecular chaperone\n\nactivity of HIV-1 NCps on the formation of tetramolecular G-quadruplex\n\nwas investigated in detail. We have obtained several new insights on these\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDNA Nanotechnology Group Kyoto University (https://endo.icems.kyoto-u.ac.jp/research_e.html)\nciteturn140search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.5 years ago; Using DNA origami frame as a nanoscaffold for the structural control, we present the unprecedented solution-state structures of a tetramolecular antiparallel and (3+1)-type G-quadruplex intermediates such as G-hairpin and G-triplex visualized directly at single-molecule level with nanometer resolution. ... We describe the direct visualization and single-molecule analysis of the formation of a tetramolecular G-quadruplex in KCl solution. ... ### Regulation of B-Z conformational transition and Zαβ protein binding by introduction of constraint to double-stranded DNA using a DNA nanoscaffold.\n\n### Direct and Single-Molecule Visualization of the Solution-State Structures of G-Hairpin and G-Triplex Intermediates.\n\nAngew. Che. Int. Ed. 53, 4107-4012 (2014)\n\nImage\nUsing DNA origami frame as a nanoscaffold for the structural control, we present the unprecedented solution-state structures of a tetramolecular antiparallel and (3+1)-type G-quadruplex intermediates such as G-hairpin and G-triplex visualized directly at single-molecule level with nanometer resolution. These intermediates are stable and formed with relatively good yield. Finally, we proposed a possible model of G-quadruplex folding.\n\n### HIV-1 Nucleocapsid Proteins as Molecular Chaperones for Tetramolecular Antiparallel G‑Quadruplex Formation.\n\nJ. Am. Chem. Soc. 135, 18575−18585 (2013)\n\nImage\nHIV-1 nucleocapsid proteins (NCps) facilitate remodeling of nucleic acids to fold thermodynamically stable conformations, and thus called nucleic acid chaperones. To date only little is known on the stoichiometry, NCp−NCp interactions, chaperone activity on G-quadruplex formation, and so on. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nControlling the stoichiometry and strand polarity of a tetramolecular G-quadruplex structure by using a DNA origami frame | Nucleic Acids Research | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/nar/article/41/18/8738/1030561)\nciteturn140search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; Here, we describe the direct visualization and single-molecule analysis of the formation of a tetramolecular G-quadruplex in KCl solution. ... The formation of the quadruplex can affect a wide range of biological activities including genome stability, cell growth, gene regulation, transcription, translation, DNA replication and DNA repair (27). ... We have recently reported the real-time analyses on a (3 + 1) type G-quadruplex structure (17) and a theoretical analysis of the folding pathways of human telomeric type-1 and type-2 G-quadruplex structures (5). ... Thus, we have performed our analyses within a nanovessel (46,47) constructed using the origami method. ... However, in our single-molecule analysis using DNA origami, we could successfully control the stoichiometry of the strands and study exclusively the single G-quadruplex of interest rather than a mixture. ... The origami was prepared and immobilized on mica surface in a buffer containing 100 mM KCl, whereas the observation buffer contained no KCl.\n## Abstract\n\nGuanine-rich oligonucleotides often show a strong tendency to form supramolecular architecture, the so-called G-quadruplex structure. Because of the biological significance, it is now considered to be one of the most important conformations of DNA. Here, we describe the direct visualization and single-molecule analysis of the formation of a tetramolecular G-quadruplex in KCl solution. The conformational changes were carried out by incorporating two duplex DNAs, with G–G mismatch repeats in the middle, inside a DNA origami frame and monitoring the topology change of the strands. In the absence of KCl, incorporated duplexes had no interaction and laid parallel to each other. Addition of KCl induced the formation of a G-quadruplex structure by stably binding the duplexes to each other in the middle. Such a quadruplex formation allowed the DNA synapsis without disturbing the duplex regions of the participating sequences, and resulted in an X-shaped structure that was monitored by atomic force microscopy. ### Real-time observation of the salt-induced formation of a G-quadruplex\n\nOne of the major goals for scientists in various disciplines is to analyze the biological reactions and functions in real-time (60–62), which could offer new insights. Real-time analysis is not possible with most available techniques, and HS-AFM is one of the few techniques available (49). Although the real-time analysis described below does not provide all the possible information, it is in fact a good initiation.\n\nTo visualize directly the formation of a single G-quadruplex in real-time, we selected the long duplex with six contiguous guanines as a representative example. The duplexes-incorporated origami frame was prepared, purified and adsorbed onto a freshly cleaved mica plate in the absence of any added K^{+}. The sample was scanned in an observation buffer that contained 100 mM K^{+}, and the image acquisition frequency was 0.2 frame/s. The results of the HS-AFM imaging are summarized in Figure 4a, and the real-time movie is given in Supplementary Movie S1. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n戦略的創造研究推進事業 ＣＲＥＳＴ (https://projectdb.jst.go.jp/file/JST-PROJECT-08062241/JST_1111056_08062241_2014_%E6%9D%89%E5%B1%B1_PER.pdf)\nciteturn140search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Direct Observation of G-Quadruplexes using \nDNA Origami Nanoscaffold. ... Direct Analysis of Holliday Junction Resolving Enzyme in a DNA Origami\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRecent Developments of New DNA Origami Nanostructures for Drug Delivery | Bentham Science (https://www.benthamscience.com/article/67789)\nciteturn140search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Recent Developments of New DNA Origami Nanostructures for Drug Delivery ... This review focuses on the development in the new scaffold design approaches, folding conditions, various nano objects functionali